#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "c1dcb0a7-441f-5628-ba23-71d43d9ea96c" :question/math-academy-id "e-495" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int x^2\\cos(3x) \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int x\\sin(3x) \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{27}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2{C}_{1}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}((9{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{2C_1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "b81ff533-2067-5d1a-b265-df1ad91fa8da" :question/math-academy-id "e-496" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^e 32x^3(\\ln x)^2 \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=32{x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 32{x}^{3}\\text{d}x=8{x}^{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(8{x}^{4})-\\int (8{x}^{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int {x^3\\ln(x)} \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is an integral constant. So, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16(\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{4}-16{C}_{1} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -4C_1.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}32{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & ={{x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[(e)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1)]-[(1)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{4}(8-4+1)]-1 \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "8ec71af3-4534-5ef1-8745-3220102ba838" :question/math-academy-id "e-7053" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (t^2+1)e^{2t+1} \\, \\textrm{d} t.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int t\\,e^{2t+1}\\,\\textrm{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-(\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -{D}.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+3))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(2-2+3)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{3e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}-3e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c50259-96ff-5091-b6d8-d010aabcd87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2}-3}{4{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247435} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1b58be-7936-591f-a3c9-e22825f21d58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248249} {:answer/id #uuid "602d95a8-3c49-5628-a5fc-26ae6d4442af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248293} {:answer/id #uuid "ae45cdbd-b5db-5833-8170-fccfe8733b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248981} {:answer/id #uuid "f16fc0ad-7654-55ab-873b-8984cf712d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{3}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae45cdbd-b5db-5833-8170-fccfe8733b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248981} :answer-field/id #uuid "46bceeef-1e66-56f1-87c4-0b40ebcc4c53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249799}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7237523-38ec-5167-93d0-30747905ffb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-114942" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=9{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 9{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=3{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}(3{x}^{3})-\\int (3{x}^{3})(\\frac{2{log}_{2}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{2}⁡x)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+D) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}{({log}_{2}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}{log}_{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}+\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{6D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{2}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}9{x}^{2}({log}_{2}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{2}{3})]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(3-2+\\frac{2}{3})-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡2}(0-0+\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}-\\frac{2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{3{ln}^{2}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97206e85-b5fa-5758-939d-0840debe9d8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}(3x^{2} - 2x + 7) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065626} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b01923-dacc-5ee7-aa33-53c34299f420" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}(6x^{2} - 3x + 1) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065627} {:answer/id #uuid "ea52abfd-2f18-593d-ad80-f97bee360e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}(x^{2} + 2x - 11) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065628} {:answer/id #uuid "67d840a8-0c83-5837-97b3-6b0f4527be00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}(3x^{2} - 6x + 11) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065629} {:answer/id #uuid "7dbbbdc4-edfe-548b-aba2-a9f23937a0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{x}(3x^{2} + 6x - 1) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065630}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a4f06b9e-a3ba-5cdd-b2de-b01063749823" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065625}] :question/id #uuid "5a014a29-8bc7-5ab9-a7b1-a2c515a94e18" :question/math-academy-id "q-12700" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int(3x^{2} + 5)e^{x}dx$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6fd4649-2cb0-50b2-a4a2-46c4c7a01fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{e}^{3}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6fd4649-2cb0-50b2-a4a2-46c4c7a01fc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15{e}^{3}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319999} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16c7d3b-53ab-5ba9-8034-5398c600be0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ebbb3ae8-e373-5e9a-af80-1375d64670c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-352122" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(${{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=81{x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 81{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=27{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}(27{x}^{3})-\\int (27{x}^{3})(\\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{54}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate  $\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{4}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}{log}_{4}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{4}⁡x)(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{54}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}(\\frac{{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+D) \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}{({log}_{4}⁡x)}^{2}-\\frac{18{x}^{3}{log}_{4}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{27{x}^{3}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{18{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡x-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{54D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nNote that in the above calculation, we used the change of base formula ${log}_{4}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}81{x}^{2}({log}_{4}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡x-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+6)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡e-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+6)-\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27{ln}^{2}⁡1-18\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(27-18+6)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(0-0+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{15{e}^{3}}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}-\\frac{6}{{ln}^{2}⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡4}(\\,15{e}^{3}-6\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
